\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n---\n\n#### Construction  \n\nConsider the three horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}: y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{2}: y=2,\\qquad \\ell_{3}: y=3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, hence by (2) none of them is sunny.  \nThus the number of sunny lines in the set \\(\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\\) is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Verification that all required points are covered  \n\nFor each point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) we check which line of (3) contains it.\n\n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{1}\\) (since \\(\\ell_{1}\\) is the set \\(\\{(x,1)\\mid x\\in\\mathbb R\\}\\)).  \n  The points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) belong to \\(\\ell_{1}\\).\n\n* If \\(b=2\\) then \\((a,2)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{2}\\).  \n  The points \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) belong to \\(\\ell_{2}\\).\n\n* If \\(b=3\\) then \\((a,3)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{3}\\).  \n  The point \\((1,3)\\) belongs to \\(\\ell_{3}\\).\n\nThus every element of the set (1) is contained in at least one of the lines (3).  \n\nSince the three lines are distinct, we have exactly three lines, all non\u2011sunny, satisfying the required covering property.\n\n---\n\n#### Conclusion  \n\nThe configuration \\(\\{\\ell_{1},\\ell_{2},\\ell_{3}\\}\\) with  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell_{1}:y=1,\\;\\ell_{2}:y=2,\\;\\ell_{3}:y=3}\n\\]\n\nprovides three distinct lines for \\(n=3\\) such that  \n\n* every integer point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le4\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* the number of sunny lines is exactly \\(k=0\\).\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines **exists**."
