inus L=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) lie on the non‑sunny line \\(y=1\\); the two points \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) lie on the non‑sunny line \\(x=2\\).  \n  Hence each sunny line can contain at most one of these five points, so two sunny lines cannot cover them all.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Diagonal non‑sunny lines (slope \\(-1\\))\n\nThe lines of the form \\(x+y=c\\) that meet \\(S\\) are  \n\n\\[\nx+y=2\\;( \\{(1,1)\\}),\\qquad\nx+y=3\\;( \\{(1,2),(2,1)\\}),\\qquad\nx+y=4\\;( \\{(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}).\n\\]\n\n* **\\(L:x+y=2\\).**  \n  Then  \n\n  \\[\n  S\\setminus L=\\{(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Any line through two of these points is either vertical, horizontal, of slope \\(-1\\) (all non‑sunny) or has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) (through \\((1,2),(3,1)\\)) or \\(-2\\) (through \\((2,1),(1,3)\\)).  \n  Suppose we use the sunny line \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)); it covers exactly those two points.  \n  The remaining three points \\((2,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\) cannot be covered by a single sunny line, because any line through two of them is either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  \n  Hence two sunny lines are insufficient.  \n  The same obstruction occurs if we start with the sunny line \\((2,1)-(1,3)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n\n* **\\(L:x+y=3\\).**  \n  Then  \n\n  \\[\n  S\\setminus L=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  The only sunny line among the pairs is \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)), which covers two points.  \n  The remaining two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on the non‑sunny line \\(x+y=4\\); they cannot be joined by a sunny line.  \n  Thus two sunny lines cannot cover all four points.\n\n* **\\(L:x+y=4\\).**  \n  Then  \n\n  \\[\n  S\\setminus L=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n\n  Any line through two of these points is vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\); all are non‑sunny.  \n  Consequentl