on\u2011sunny line \\(x=1\\); the two points \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) lie on the non\u2011sunny line \\(x=2\\).  \n> Consequently each sunny line can contain at most one of the five points, so two sunny lines cannot cover them all.\u201d\n\n**Issue \u2013 Justification Gap:**  \nThe claim \u201ceach sunny line can contain at most one of the five points\u201d is **false**. The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is sunny, and therefore a sunny line can contain **two** of the five points. The solution does not acknowledge this possibility, so the statement lacks justification and is incorrect. (Nevertheless, even allowing that sunny line, the remaining three points \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1)\\) cannot be covered by a single sunny line, so the final conclusion that two sunny lines are insufficient remains true, but the intermediate claim is unjustified.)\n\n### 6. Horizontal non\u2011sunny lines\n\n> \u201cThe horizontal lines meeting\u202f\\(S\\) are  \n> \\[\n> y=1\\;( \\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\}),\\qquad\n> y=2\\;( \\{(1,2),(2,2)\\}),\\qquad\n> y=3\\;( \\{(1,3)\\}).\n> \\]\u201d\n\n**Verification:** Correct enumeration.\n\n#### 6.1. Subcase\u202f\\(L:y=1\\)\n\n> \u201cThen  \n> \\[\n> S\\setminus L=\\{(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}.\n> \\]  \n> Any line through two of these points is respectively horizontal \\((y=2)\\), vertical \\((x=1)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny.  \n> Hence a sunny line can contain at most one of the three points, and two sunny lines are insufficient.\u201d\n\n**Verification:**  \n- \\((1,2)-(2,2)\\) gives \\(y=2\\) (horizontal).  \n- \\((1,2)-(1,3)\\) gives \\(x=1\\) (vertical).  \n- \\((2,2)-(1,3)\\) gives slope \\((3-2)/(1-2)=1/(-1)=-1\\).  \nAll are non\u2011sunny, so each sunny line can cover at most one point. Two sunny lines cannot cover three points. Correct.\n\n#### 6.2. Subcase\u202f\\(L:y=2\\)\n\n> \u201cThen  \n> \\[\n> S\\setminus L=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,3),(2,2)\\}.\n> \\]  \n> The three points \\((1,1),(2,1