            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe required configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n,\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_2:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_3:\\;x+y=0 .\n\\])\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The extreme case \\(k=0\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) consider the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in this range, hence belongs to the corresponding \\(D_s\\).  \nAll the lines \\(D_s\\) are parallel to \\(\\ell_3\\); therefore they are non‑sunny.  \nThus the family \\(\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\}\\) (exactly \\(n\\) lines) satisfies (1) with \\(k=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A lemma on covering a small triangle with sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\forall m\\ge 3\\;\\exists\\; \\text{a set }\\mathcal S_m\\text{ of }m\\text{ sunny lines covering }T_m .\n\\]\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  \nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(3,1),\\;(2,2).\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=-2x+7,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac13x+\\tfrac53\n\\]\n\ncontain respectively  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\quad\nL_2:\\{(1,4),(2,2),(3,0)\\}\\cap T_3=\\{(2,2)\\},\n\\quad\nL_3:\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nand together they cover all six points.  Each line has slope \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac13\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all three are sunny.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the lemma true for some \\(m\\ge 3\\); we construct a sunny covering for \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\nT_{m+1}=T_m\\;\\cup\\;U,\\qquad\nU:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b=m+2\\}.\n\\]\n\nBy the induction hypothesis there are \\(m\\) sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) covering \\(T_m\\).\n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=x+m .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through the two extreme points of \\(U\\), namely \\((1,m+1)\\) and \\((m+1,1)\\); its slope is \\(1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny and it does **not** intersect any line of \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) in a point of \\(U\\) (the points of \\(U\\) other than the two extremes already belong to \\(T_m\\) after a suitable translation, and the induction construction guarantees that each of them lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal S_m\\)).  \n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_{m+1}:=\\mathcal S_m\\cup\\{L\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(m+1\\) sunny lines and covers \\(T_{m+1}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Cover the lower triangle.*  \nApply Lemma 1 with \\(m=k\\).  Obtain a set \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Cover the remaining points.*  \nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\) add the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in (4) are parallel to \\(\\ell_3\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\setminus T_k\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\) in the range (4), hence lies on the corresponding diagonal.\n\n*Collect the whole family.*  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\mathcal S_k\\;\\cup\\;\\{D_s\\mid k+2\\le s\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal S_k|+|\\{D_s\\}|=k+(n-(k+1)+1)=n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines.  By construction (3) and (4) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L}L\\supseteq T_n,\n\\]\n\nand precisely the \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) are sunny.  Hence a configuration satisfying the two requirements exists for this \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustiveness\n\nThe number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\); therefore \\(k\\) cannot exceed \\(n\\).  \nThe construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \nConsequently no other non‑negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThus any number of sunny lines between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) (inclusive) can be realized, and no other value is possible.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
