only point of \\(S_n\\) not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\).  \n  Add the line through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); its equation is\n  \\[\n  y-n=(1-n)(x-1)\\qquad\\bigl(y=(1-n)x+2n-1\\bigr).\n  \\]\n  Its slope \\(1-n\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (because \\(n\\ge3\\)), so the line is sunny.  \n  The resulting family consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly one of which is sunny, and it covers all of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 3.  Constructions with three sunny lines for the small values \\(n=3,4\\)\n\n* **\\(n=3\\).**  \n  The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points\n  \\[\n  (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n  \\]\n  Consider the three sunny lines\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n  One checks directly:\n  \\[\n  L_1\\cap S_3=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\;\n  L_2\\cap S_3=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\;\n  L_3\\cap S_3=\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n  \\]\n  Hence the three lines cover all points of \\(S_3\\) and each of them is sunny; therefore \\(k=3\\) is attainable for \\(n=3\\).\n\n* **\\(n=4\\).**  \n  The set \\(S_4\\) consists of the ten points\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),\\\\\n  &(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),\\\\\n  &(3,1),(3,2),\\\\\n  &(4,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  The four lines\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\ell_1:\\;x=1 &&\\text{(non‑sunny)},\\\\\n  &\\ell_2:\\;y=x-1 &&\\text{(sunny, slope }1),\\\\\n  &\\ell_3:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+3 &&\\text{(sunny, slope }-\\tfrac12),\\\\\n  &\\ell_4:\\;y=-2x+7 &&\\text{(sunny, slope }-2)\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  contain respectively\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &\\ell_1\\cap S_4=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4)\\},\\\\\n  &\\ell_2\\cap S_4=\\{(2,1),(3,2)\\},\\\\\n  &\\ell_3\\cap S_4=\\{(2,2),(4,1)\\},\\\\\n  &\\ell_4\\cap S_4=\\{(2,3),(3,1)\\}.\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  Every point of \\(S_4\\) appears in one of the four intersections, so the family covers \\(S_4\\) and contains exactly three sunny lines. Hence \\(k=3\\) is also attainable for \\(n=4\\).\n\n#### 4.  Non‑existence of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly two sunny lines, say \\(L_1\\) and \\(L_2\\).  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N=\\mathcal F\\setminus\\{L_1,L_2\\}\\); then \\(|\\mathcal N|=n-2\\) and every line in \\(\\mathcal N\\) is non‑sunny (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.** *A sunny line meets each non‑sunny line in at most one point of \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n*Proof.*  \nTwo distinct lines intersect in at most one point. Because the slopes are different, the intersection point cannot be a whole segment; therefore the intersection consists of a single point, which may or may not belong to \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\nConsequently each sunny line can contain at most one point from each line of \\(\\mathcal N\\). In addition the two sunny lines may intersect each other, providing at most one extra point of \\(S_n\\). Hence\n\n\\[\n|L_1\\cap S_n|+|L_2\\cap S_n|\n\\le 2\\bigl(|\\mathcal N|+1\\bigr)=2\\bigl((n-2)+1\\bigr)=2n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.** *Every non‑sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).*  \n\n*Proof.*  \nA horizontal line \\(y=c\\) (with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\)) meets the triangle in the segment \\(\\{(a,c)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\}\\), which has exactly \\(n+1-c\\le n\\) integer points.  \nA vertical line \\(x=c\\) is analogous.  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\); intersecting the triangle gives the integer points \\((a,d-a)\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le d-1\\) and \\(d\\le n+1\\), i.e. at most \\(d-1\\le n\\) points. ∎\n\nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal N}|L\\cap S_n|\\le (n-2)\\,n. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\nAdding (1) and (2) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal F\\):\n\n\\[\n|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F} (L\\cap S_n)|\n\\le (n-2)n+(2n-2)=n^{2}-2. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow compare this bound with the actual size of \\(S_n\\):\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) we have\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}-2,\n\\]\nand for \\(n\\ge5\\) we improve the estimate of Lemma 2 as follows.  \nIn the triangle the longest horizontal (or vertical) line contains exactly \\(n\\) points, but one of those points must already belong to a sunny line (otherwise the two sunny lines would be parallel to that longest line, contradicting their sunniness). Hence each non‑sunny line can contribute at most \\(n-1\\) new points, giving the sharper bound\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{L\\in\\mathcal N}|L\\cap S_n|\\le (n-2)(n-1). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nCombining (1) with (4) yields\n\n\\[\n|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal F} (L\\cap S_n)|\n\\le (n-2)(n-1)+(2n-2)=n^{2}-n. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2>n^{2}-n,\n\\]\nbecause the inequality is equivalent to \\(n>2\\). Thus the number of points that can be covered by a family with exactly two sunny lines is strictly smaller than \\(|S_n|\\). This contradiction shows that **no** covering of \\(S_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n∎\n\n#### 5.  Summary of what has been proved  \n\n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable.  \n* For \\(n=3\\) and \\(n=4\\) the value \\(k=3\\) is also realizable (explicit families are given).  \n* For every \\(n\\ge3\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \n\nThe existence (or impossibility) of values \\(k\\ge4\\) when \\(n\\ge5\\) has not been settled by the arguments presented here. Further work would be required to either construct such families or to extend the counting method of Lemma 1–Lemma 2 to obtain stronger upper bounds."
