            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **non‑sunny** if it is horizontal \\((y=c)\\), vertical\n\\((x=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound on the number of sunny lines  \n\nWe must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, therefore we can never have more\nthan \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Consequently any admissible value of \\(k\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  We construct a family \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny backbone  \n\nInclude the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_1:\\;x=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(V_1\\) is non‑sunny and contains every point of the first column of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:=\\text{the line through }(1,i+1)\\text{ and }(i+1,n).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+(i+1)=i+2\\le n+1\\).\nThe second point \\((i+1,n)\\) may lie outside the triangle; this is irrelevant,\nonly the fact that the line is well defined matters.\n\nThe slope of \\(S_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{n-(i+1)}{i}= \\frac{n-i-1}{i}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(0\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n* \\(m_i\\neq0\\) because \\(n-i-1\\neq0\\) (otherwise \\(i=n-1\\) and then\n  \\(m_i=0\\) would give a horizontal line, but \\(i=n-1\\) would imply\n  \\(k\\ge n-1\\); in that case we replace the line \\(S_{n-1}\\) by the line\n  through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,1)\\), which has slope \\(1-n\\neq0\\).)\n* \\(m_i\\) is finite, so the line is not vertical.\n* \\(m_i=-1\\) would give \\(n-i-1 = -i\\), i.e. \\(n=1\\), impossible because\n  \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  The slopes \\(m_i\\) are distinct (the numerator\ndecreases while the denominator increases), so the lines \\(S_i\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The remaining horizontal lines  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nH:=\\{\\,b\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid b\\neq i+1\\text{ for all }1\\le i\\le k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor every \\(b\\in H\\) add the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause the set \\(\\{i+1\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) has exactly \\(k\\) elements,\n\\(|H|=n-k\\); therefore we add precisely \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\n\\mathcal F_k =\\{V_1\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\cup\\{H_b\\}_{b\\in H}}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncontains  \n\n\\[\n1\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-k)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\n= n\n\\]\n\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.