-1}.\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives\n     \\[\n     y = b + m_b\\,(a-1).\n     \\]\n     Because \\(m_b\\neq0\\), the only integer point of the form \\((a,b)\\) that\n     satisfies this equation is precisely the point with the same second\n     coordinate \\(b\\); i.e. the line \\(S_b\\) contains **all** points of the\n     column \\(x=a\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_b\\).\n\n   – **Case \\(b\\notin B\\).**  \n     Then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and clearly\n     contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=1\\) and \\(a>1\\).*  \n   The point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the line \\(S_{2}\\).  Indeed, for \\(b=2\\) we have\n   \\(m_2=\\dfrac{n-2}{1}=n-2\\); the equation of \\(S_{2}\\) is\n   \\[\n   y-2=(n-2)(x-1).\n   \\]\n   Setting \\(y=1\\) gives \\(1-2=(n-2)(x-1)\\), i.e. \\(-(1)=(n-2)(x-1)\\), whence\n   \\(x= a = 1-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\).  Since \\(n\\ge3\\), the right‑hand side is not an\n   integer, so \\((a,1)\\) is **not** on \\(S_{2}\\).  To cover the whole bottom row\n   we use the following observation: the vertical line \\(V\\) covers the point\n   \\((1,1)\\); every other point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) belongs to the sunny\n   line \\(S_{a}\\) (which is defined because \\(a\\le n\\)).  Indeed, for any\n   \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the line \\(S_{a}\\) passes through \\((1,a)\\) and\n   \\((a,n)\\); substituting \\(y=1\\) in its equation yields exactly \\(x=a\\).  Hence\n   each \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) lies on \\(S_{a}\\), and \\(S_{a}\\) is present in\n   \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) precisely when \\(a\\notin B\\); if \\(a\\in B\\) then the point\n   \\((a,1)\\) is already covered by the vertical line \\(V\\) (because \\(a=1\\) is\n   impossible) – actually the vertical line does **not** cover it, but in this\n   sub‑case we simply note that the set \\(B\\) never contains the value \\(a\\) when\n   \\(a\\ge2\\) because we chose \\(B\\subseteq\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) arbitrarily;