le with only \\(n-1\\) lines left.  Therefore a\n   covering family of size \\(n\\) can contain **at most \\(n-1\\) sunny lines**,\n   i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n2. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   We start from the obvious covering consisting of the vertical line\n   \\[\n   V:\\;x=1\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   and the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=2,3,\\dots ,n).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   This family \\(\\mathcal F_0=\\{V\\}\\cup\\{H_b\\}_{b=2}^{n}\\) has \\(n\\) lines,\n   all non‑sunny, and clearly covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   To obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines we replace \\(k\\) of the horizontal\n   lines \\(H_{b}\\) (with distinct values of \\(b\\)) by \\(k\\) sunny lines that\n   still pass through the same points \\((1,b)\\).  For each integer\n   \\(b\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line\n   \\[\n   S_b:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,b)\\text{ and }(b,n).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   The second point \\((b,n)\\) lies on the line \\(x+y=n+b\\), which is outside the\n   triangle \\(T_n\\); its only purpose is to fix a slope.  The slope of \\(S_b\\) is\n   \\[\n   m_b=\\frac{n-b}{\\,b-1\\,}.\n   \\]\n   Because \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(2\\le b\\le n\\), we have \\(m_b\\neq0\\), \\(m_b\\) is finite,\n   and \\(m_b\\neq-1\\); consequently each \\(S_b\\) is **sunny**.  Moreover the\n   collection \\(\\{S_b\\mid b\\in B\\}\\) for distinct \\(b\\)’s consists of distinct\n   lines (different slopes).\n\n   Fix a set \\(B\\subseteq\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|B|=k\\).  Replace the\n   horizontal lines \\(H_b\\) for \\(b\\in B\\) by the sunny lines \\(S_b\\).  The\n   resulting family\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;\n   \\mathcal F_k=\n   \\{V\\}\\cup\\{S_b\\}_{b\\in B}\\cup\\{H_b\\}_{b\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus B}\n   \\;}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   contains exactly\n   \\[\n   1\\;(\\text{vertical})\\;+\\;k\\;(\\text{sunny})\\;+\\;(n-1-k)\\;(\\text{horizontal})\n   = n\n   \\]\n   distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Verification of the covering property.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a=1\\)*, the point lies on the vertical line \\(V\\).\n\n   *If \\(a>1\\)*, then \\(b\\ge2\\) (otherwise \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) would give\n   \\(a\\le n\\) and the point \\((a,1)\\) would have \\(a\\ge2\\); such a point is\n   handled below).  For \\(b\\ge2\\) we consider whether \\(b\\) belongs to the set\n   \\(B\\).\n\n   – **Case \\(b\\in B\\).**  \n     By definition the line \\(S_b\\) passes through \\((1,b)\\) and \\((b,n)\\); its\n     equation is\n     \\[\n     y-b = m_b\\,(x-1),\\qquad m_b=\\frac{n-b}{b-1}.\n     \\]\n     Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives\n     \\[\n     y = b + m_b\\,(a-1).\n     \\]\n     Because \\(m_b\\neq0\\), the only integer point of the form \\((a,b)\\) that\n     satisfies this equation is precisely the point with the same second\n     coordinate \\(b\\); i.e. the line \\(S_b\\) contains **all** points of the\n     column \\(x=a\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(b\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_b\\).\n\n   – **Case \\(b\\notin B\\).**  \n     Then the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) and clearly\n     contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\n   *If \\(b=1\\) and \\(a>1\\).*  \n   The point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the line \\(S_{2}\\).  Indeed, for \\(b=2\\) we have\n   \\(m_2=\\dfrac{n-2}{1}=n-2\\); the equation of \\(S_{2}\\) is\n   \\[\n   y-2=(n-2)(x-1).\n   \\]\n   Setting \\(y=1\\) gives \\(1-2=(n-2)(x-1)\\), i.e. \\(-(1)=(n-2)(x-1)\\), whence\n   \\(x= a = 1-\\dfrac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\).  Since \\(n\\ge3\\), the right‑hand side is not an\n   integer, so \\((a,1)\\) is **not** on \\(S_{2}\\).  To cover the whole bottom row\n   we use the following observation: the vertical line \\(V\\) covers the point\n   \\((1,1)\\); every other point \\((a,1)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) belongs to the sunny\n   line \\(S_{a}\\) (which is defined because \\(a\\le n\\)).  Indeed, for any\n   \\(a\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the 