guration for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution (partial)**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  Hence the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Reduction to the case \u201call lines sunny\u201d\n\nAssume that for the given \\(n\\) we can exhibit a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) **distinct sunny** lines that together cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf such a family exists, then for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can obtain a required configuration as follows.\n\nChoose arbitrarily a subset \\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|U|=n-k\\).  \nReplace each sunny line \\(S_i\\) with \\(i\\in U\\) by the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{i+1}:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\n(The diagonal lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.)  \nDenote the resulting family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\,S_i\\mid i\