"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for the case \u201call\u202f\\(n\\) lines are sunny\u2019\u2019 (i.e. \\(k=n\\)).  The argument given in the original solution for this case was incorrect, and a correct covering with \\(n\\) sunny lines has not been established.  Consequently the answer \u201call integers \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are possible\u2019\u2019 cannot be claimed with certainty.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n*Upper bound.*  Because exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines must be used, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\); thus \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*Construction for \\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\nare all non\u2011sunny (they are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis).  Every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), hence lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Therefore the case \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n*Partial progress toward larger \\(k\\).*  Replacing some of the vertical lines by the diagonal lines  \n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c\\qquad(c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1)\n\\]\n(which are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny) yields configurations with any prescribed number of non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) **provided** we already have a family of \\(n\\) sunny lines to start from.  The original solution claimed such a family exists (the lines \\(y=jx-(j-1)\\), \\(j=1,\\dots ,n\\)), but the verification that these lines cover all points of the set\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\nwas incorrect.\n\n*Remaining open task.*  A correct explicit construction (or a proof of impossibility) for a covering of \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines is still required.  Once such a construction is available, the \u201cinterpolation\u2019\u2019 argument used in the original solution (replace any \\(n-k\\) sunny lines by diagonal lines) would give a valid configuration for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution (partial)**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound\n\nSince we must use exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines:\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none of them is sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  Thus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.  Hence the value \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Reduction to the case \u201call lines sunny\u201d\n\nAssume that for the given \\(n\\) we can exhibit a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) **distinct sunny** lines that together cover every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nIf such a family exists, then for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we can obtain a required configuration as follows.\n\nChoose arbitrarily a subset \\(U\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(|U|=n-k\\).  \nReplace each sunny line \\(S_i\\) with \\(i\\in U\\) by the diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_{i+1}:\\;x+y=i+1 .\n\\]\n\n(The diagonal lines are parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.)  \nDenote the resulting family by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_k=\n\\bigl\\{\\,S_i\\mid i\\notin U\\,\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{\\,D_{i+1}\\mid i\\in U\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines (the retained \\(S_i\\)\u2019s) and \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Covering property.*  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf the diagonal line \\(D_c\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal F_k\\) (i.e. if \\(c-1\\in U\\)), the point is covered.  \nOtherwise \\(c-1\\notin U\\), so the sunny line \\(S_{c-1}\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal F_k\\); by the assumption on \\(\\mathcal S\\) this line contains the point \\((a,b)\\).  Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\nTherefore, **provided** a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines exists, the values \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n\\) are all attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Status of the \u201call\u2011sunny\u2019\u2019 covering\n\nThe original solution claimed that the lines  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\;y=j\\,x-(j-1)\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nform such a covering.  The verification given there was incorrect: the equation\n\\(b=j\\,a-(j-1)\\) does **not** hold for a general point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); consequently many points of \\(T_n\\) are not on any of the lines \\(S_j\\).\n\nAt present I have not succeeded in producing a correct explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\), nor have I proved that such a family cannot exist.  This is the missing ingredient needed to complete the classification of all admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion (partial)\n\n* The trivial upper bound \\(k\\le n\\) holds for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n* The value \\(k=0\\) is realizable (by the \\(n\\) vertical lines).\n* If a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines can be exhibited, then **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is realizable by the replacement procedure described in \u00a73.\n\nThe existence (or non\u2011existence) of a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines remains an open sub\u2011problem; resolving it would complete the determination of all admissible \\(k\\). \u220e"
